WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.320
All right.

00:04.320 --> 00:08.240
So that was the first part of the window definition within the over clause.

00:08.280 --> 00:09.480
Let's move to the next part.

00:09.520 --> 00:13.200
We have the order by for example we can use order by order date.

00:13.200 --> 00:14.320
It's just a field.

00:14.560 --> 00:19.000
So the order clause is very important in order to sort your data within a window.

00:19.240 --> 00:22.440
So the order by is very important as well for many functions.

00:22.440 --> 00:26.600
So by just checking the overview over here for the aggregate functions it is optional.

00:26.600 --> 00:28.480
So you could just leave it or add it.

00:28.680 --> 00:33.800
But for the rank function and as well for the value functions they are a must.

00:33.800 --> 00:39.440
So if you want to use those functions you must use the order clause because it makes no sense.

00:39.440 --> 00:43.320
For example, if you are ranking the data without sorting your data first.

00:43.360 --> 00:43.880
Okay guys.

00:43.880 --> 00:45.440
So now back to our very simple example.

00:45.440 --> 00:46.800
And we have the following query.

00:46.800 --> 00:48.640
So the function this time is going to be ranked.

00:48.640 --> 00:50.440
So we have to rank the data.

00:50.440 --> 00:53.640
And the definition of the window is going to be partitioned by month.

00:53.640 --> 00:56.280
So that means we divide the data by the months.

00:56.280 --> 00:57.760
So we have it over here.

00:58.000 --> 01:01.640
And then the second part is going to be order by sales descending.

01:01.640 --> 01:04.970
So we have to sort each window by descending order.

01:04.970 --> 01:08.890
That means we start with the highest value and we end up by the lowest value.

01:09.010 --> 01:11.570
So let's see how SQL is going to go and execute this.

01:11.890 --> 01:13.330
So first partitioned by month.

01:13.330 --> 01:18.130
So it's going to divide the data into two partitions because we have two values by the month.

01:18.330 --> 01:20.690
So let's see how this is going to look like.

01:20.770 --> 01:25.010
So one window for January and another window for February.

01:25.090 --> 01:25.450
All right.

01:25.450 --> 01:29.770
So now SQL can go to the second part and execute order by sales descending.

01:29.930 --> 01:30.770
So what's going to happen.

01:30.810 --> 01:36.050
SQL is going to go for each window separately and start sorting the data from the highest to the lowest

01:36.050 --> 01:37.890
without checking the other window.

01:38.090 --> 01:41.050
So in those three values the highest one is this one.

01:41.050 --> 01:42.170
So it's going to be on top.

01:42.170 --> 01:43.290
Let me just sort it.

01:43.330 --> 01:46.410
This is going to be the lowest you're going to be in the middle.

01:46.570 --> 01:50.490
So SQL is going to sort this window separately from the next one.

01:50.490 --> 01:53.050
And then once it's done it's going to go to the second one.

01:53.050 --> 01:55.410
So the highest value is going to be this one.

01:55.570 --> 01:57.090
You are the lowest.

01:57.210 --> 01:58.650
Let me just do it like this.

01:58.850 --> 02:01.330
So SQL going to sort it like this.

02:01.330 --> 02:02.570
The highest one is 70.

02:02.610 --> 02:05.170
The next one is 40 and the last one is five.

02:05.210 --> 02:08.130
So with that SQL done with the definition of the window.

02:08.300 --> 02:12.700
so it's splitted by the month and each window is sorted by the sales.

02:12.820 --> 02:16.060
The next step is going to go and rank those values.

02:16.060 --> 02:19.660
So it's really simple in the outputs it's going to rank the data like this.

02:19.660 --> 02:22.180
So the first one going to be this value.

02:22.180 --> 02:25.900
The next one going to be two and the third one going to be three.

02:26.060 --> 02:28.300
So as you can see it's sorting only this window.

02:28.300 --> 02:32.060
And it's going to go and repeat the same stuff for the second window.

02:32.060 --> 02:34.180
So each rank is separately from the others.

02:34.180 --> 02:35.380
So as you can see it's very simple.

02:35.380 --> 02:40.620
This is how SQL executes partition by together with the order by for the rank function.

02:40.660 --> 02:40.900
All right.

02:40.940 --> 02:42.740
So now let's have a quick task for the order by.

02:42.780 --> 02:47.580
It says rank each order based on their sales from the highest to the lowest.

02:47.580 --> 02:51.140
And we have to provide additional information like order ID and order date.

02:51.140 --> 02:53.100
So let's see how we can write the query.

02:53.100 --> 02:56.340
So we have the basic stuff order ID order date and the sales.

02:56.340 --> 02:59.660
And now we're going to go and rank the data using window function.

02:59.660 --> 03:01.860
So we're going to use the function rank.

03:02.300 --> 03:05.980
And then we're going to tell SQL this is a window function.

03:05.980 --> 03:09.180
And inside it we have now to provide the definition of the window.

03:09.180 --> 03:13.460
So now by checking the task you can see that we don't have to divide the data, so we don't have to

03:13.500 --> 03:14.460
use partition by.

03:14.660 --> 03:15.940
We have just to use rank.

03:15.940 --> 03:18.420
And with rank we have to use the order by.

03:18.460 --> 03:19.180
It is must.

03:19.180 --> 03:24.900
So we're going to use order by the field going to be the sales and from the highest to the lowest.

03:24.900 --> 03:27.220
So let's just call it rank sales.

03:27.380 --> 03:29.220
And let's go and execute this.

03:29.260 --> 03:33.020
And as you can see our result is going to be sorted from the highest to the lowest.

03:33.020 --> 03:36.740
So you can see the sales 90 at the top and the lowest going to be the ten.

03:36.780 --> 03:38.060
And as well we have a rank.

03:38.060 --> 03:40.180
So for the top rank it's going to be one.

03:40.180 --> 03:41.900
And the lowest rank going to be ten.

03:41.900 --> 03:45.060
So as you can see we just quickly create a rank in SQL.

03:45.100 --> 03:46.020
It's very simple.

03:46.180 --> 03:49.900
The whole thing is one window since we are not using partition P.

03:49.940 --> 03:55.580
And of course if you want to have ascending so from the lowest to the highest, you can just remove

03:55.580 --> 03:57.900
it because optionally you're going to be ascending.

03:57.900 --> 04:00.020
So let's go and execute the query.

04:00.060 --> 04:02.940
So now we can see the orders are sorted the way around.

04:02.940 --> 04:05.380
So we start with the lowest and end up with the highest.

04:05.380 --> 04:09.460
And of course you're going to get the same results if you go over here and add ascending.

04:09.700 --> 04:13.300
So if we execute you see we got exactly the same results.

04:13.300 --> 04:16.660
So this is how you use the order by inside the window definition.
